# A Universe of Knowledge

**`knowledge` is the epistemic universe over which HQL reasons.** It is not the
set of facts believed to be true, and it is not merely the graph formed by HMD
links. It is the entire space of epistemically relevant material: claims,
evidence, relations, constraints, proofs, contradictions, derivations,
certifications, and assessments.

```text
knowledge : Universe<Concept>
```

Conflicting claims may coexist inside it. Evidence may support or weaken them.
Constraints may reveal that they cannot all hold. Inference may derive new
claims from the ones already present. Proofs and certifications may establish
stronger forms of justification. Trust is therefore not necessarily a property
stored on an individual claim, but an assessment emerging from that claim's
position in the whole environment.

The atomic addressable unit of this universe is a [[concept|Concept]], which has
its own card. This card is about the universe those concepts populate.

**The HQL in this card is illustrative.** The surface syntax belongs to the HQL
design and is not frozen here; what this card commits to is the shape of the
universe those queries run over.

## What the universe contains

The universe is populated by epistemic material of several kinds:

```text
knowledge
├── entities and ideas
├── claims
├── evidence
├── relations
├── constraints
├── proofs
├── derivations
├── contradictions
├── certifications
└── assessments
```

These are not fields hanging off some containing record. Each is itself a
concept — addressable, typed, and able to be claimed about in turn. A piece of
evidence can be referenced, disputed, and superseded exactly as a person can;
an assessment is a thing in the universe, not an annotation on the margin of
one.

That is what makes the epistemic structure part of the knowledge rather than
part of the machinery reading it. A store that keeps facts and treats support,
conflict, and justification as bookkeeping has put most of the interesting
material outside its own model.

## Knowledge includes uncertainty

This is the conceptual heart of the design.

The universe holds, at the same time and without contradiction *of itself*:

```text
claim(P)
claim(not P)
evidence(P)
evidence(not P)
conflict(...)
```

A universe containing both `claim(P)` and `claim(not P)` is not broken. It is
recording something true: that two assertions have been made and the matter is
not settled. Only a universe that had to choose one of them in order to store
either would be losing information.

Therefore:

```text
knowledge != set of accepted truths
```

Accepted truth is something you can *ask* this universe for. It is not what the
universe is made of.

## The investigation before its resolution

Suppose two men each claim to be Alice's father.

```text
John claims fatherOf(john, alice)
Bob  claims fatherOf(bob, alice)

constraint: a person has at most one biological father

evidence supports one claim, or the other, or both
```

Every line of that is knowledge. The two claims, the constraint they cannot
jointly satisfy, the evidence bearing on each, and the conflict the constraint
exposes are all concepts in the universe, held simultaneously.

Nothing here has been resolved, and the representation does not suffer for it.
A model that could only store the outcome would have nothing to say until the
question was already answered — and would be unable to record how it came to be
answered, or to revise the answer when a later document arrives.

The knowledge universe represents the investigation, not only its verdict.

## Reasoning derives new concepts

The universe is not passive storage. Its material combines to produce more of
itself:

```text
claims + evidence + rules + constraints
    -> contradictions
    -> inferred claims
    -> assessments
    -> proofs
    -> certifications
```

A constraint meeting two claims yields a contradiction that no author wrote
down. An assessment weighing evidence yields a judgement that was not asserted
by anyone. Each of those is a concept like any other, and each may in turn be
supported, disputed, or proved.

So the universe evolves under reasoning rather than only under authoring. What
is in it is not limited to what somebody typed into a card.

## Trust is a function of the universe

Trust is one of the motivating reasons for modelling knowledge this way.

The tempting shape is a field:

```text
claim.trust
```

The accurate shape is a function:

```text
trust = f(knowledge)
```

How much a claim deserves to be believed depends on who asserted it, what
evidence bears on it, which constraints touch it, what contradicts it, whether
anything proves it, and what is known about the reliability of every one of
those — in other words, on the claim's position in the whole environment.

A number stapled to the claim cannot answer that, because the answer changes
when nothing about the claim changes. New evidence elsewhere in the universe
raises or lowers it. An assessment is the universe's answer at a moment, and it
is itself a concept that can be recorded, cited, and disagreed with.

## Accepted knowledge is a projection

Because the universe is larger than what is believed, what is believed is a
*view* of it.

```hql
knowledge
| accepted
```

is not equivalent to `knowledge`. It is one projection, and there are others
over the same material:

```hql
knowledge
| disputed
```

```hql
knowledge
| contradicted
```

```hql
knowledge
| proven
```

Each answers a different epistemic question, and none of them is the universe.
Reading a projection as the whole is the mistake this model exists to prevent:
`accepted` silently discards every open question, and a system that only ever
shows you `accepted` cannot tell you that it did.

## A graph is one projection among many

Graph structure is subordinate to knowledge, not identical with it.

```hql
knowledge
| graph
```

should be read as: *project some part of this epistemic universe as a graph*.
Which part is a choice, and different choices give genuinely different graphs
over the same universe:

```text
entity graph
claim graph
evidence graph
contradiction graph
proof graph
trust graph
```

The links an author writes between cards form one of these and only one. Taking
that graph to *be* the knowledge is the position this card rejects — it is a
projection that keeps the relations somebody chose to write down and drops the
claims, the evidence, the constraints, and every conflict between them.

## Definition

> The **knowledge universe** is the population of concepts recognized by an HQL
> environment together with the typed epistemic structure relating them:
> everything that may be asserted, questioned, supported, contradicted,
> inferred, constrained, assessed, proved, or certified.

Or more compactly:

```text
knowledge = concepts + epistemic structure
```

Everything narrower — the accepted facts, the authored graph, the settled
answers — is something the universe can be asked for.
